>>>>> "cc" == chuckcar  <chuckcar@nil.org> writes:

        (quoting the GPL, from several posts back)

    cc> A program is free software if users have all of these
    cc> freedoms. Thus, you should be free to redistribute copies,
    cc> either with or without modifications, either gratis or
    cc> charging a fee for distribution, to anyone anywhere. 

    cc> *If* you *own* the software isn't it? otherwise you can't sell
    cc> something you don't own, right? That's my point, GNU owns
    cc> bash, *not* Mcrosoft.

The part of the GPL that you quote *explicitly* gives me the right to
redistribute copies for a fee.  It places no constraints on what the
fee I may charge is, either.  The part of the license that requires me
to provide source code *does* place limits on what I can charge for
that service, but that's not what we're talking about.

In other words, if I ask a $100,000 distribution fee for bash, and you
choose to pay it, neither of us is in violation of the license,
especially if I throw in a copy of the source code on a $2 CDROM.  If
you pay it, and then redistribute that copy of bash to all your
friends for free, you're still not in violation of the license.  If
one of your friends then decides to charge $1.5 million for a copy of
bash, he or she is still not in violation of the license.

Of course, you can get bash for free, so there's no reason to pay me
$100,000 or your friend $1.5 million.  But there's nothing in the
license that prohibits it.

